# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Notebooks
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# `*.ipynb` with no slash in it, matched at any depth. This rule previously read
# `notebooks/*.ipynb`, and the Phase 14 restructure silently killed it: a pattern
# containing a slash is anchored to the directory holding the .gitignore, so once
# the folder became `backend/notebooks/` the rule matched nothing and a committed
# notebook — outputs, embedded data and all — would have gone straight in.
#
# This is the mirror image of the `/data/` bug fixed in efff6ce. That pattern had
# no leading slash and matched too much, excluding source code. This one had a
# slash and matched too little. Both failures are silent, and both were found by
# testing the rule against a real path rather than reading it.
#
# The intent is "notebook files are not a deliverable here", which is a statement
# about the file type and not about one directory, so the rule no longer names a
# directory at all.
*.ipynb

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Node and Next.js
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Lockfiles are NOT ignored. `npm ci` in CI needs package-lock.json, and a
# reproducible frontend build is the same argument as a seeded dataset.
node_modules/
.next/
out/
next-env.d.ts
.turbo/
*.tsbuildinfo
.vercel/
npm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*

# Vitest coverage output, mirroring the Python side.
/frontend/coverage/

# Local overrides only. .env.example stays committed as documentation.
.env*.local

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local tool configuration
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `.claude/settings.local.json` was committed, and it should not have been. The
# filename says local, and the contents were machine-specific: absolute paths to
# `D:/miniconda3/envs/guardmatch/Scripts/...`. Anyone cloning the repository
# inherited permission rules pointing at an interpreter that does not exist on
# their machine — noise at best, and a confusing failure at worst.
#
# A shared `.claude/settings.json` would be a reasonable thing to commit. This
# file is the deliberately un-shared one.
.claude/settings.local.json

